{
  "turn": 3,
  "input": "Iris, what sort of company do you consistently dislike, and what makes sharing a room easier for you? I am asking about your ordinary preference, not just this moment.",
  "first_physical": 6,
  "last_physical": 7,
  "outcome": {
    "ok": true,
    "turn_id": "d09_unique_value:r4:t3",
    "narration": "Iris shifted on the edge of the bed, considering the question with the same unhurried attention she'd given the others. The room around them stayed quiet — bare wardrobes, empty cabinets, two patient stations waiting for a purpose they didn't yet have.\n\n\"I can tell you what I've seen of myself, at least,\" she said. \"I don't take to people who press and press until they get what they want, especially when they know the other person can't easily push back. Someone who notices another person has no leverage and uses that — that's the company I lose patience with.\" She paused, glancing toward the windowless stone walls as though checking the thought against something. \"As for sharing a room, it's easier when no one is watching me for a reaction. When I'm not being studied, I suppose. Someone just being *in* the space, doing their own thing — that sits fine.\"\n\nHer thumb passed lightly across her knuckles, a brief, absent gesture. \"I don't know if that's what you're asking. It's what I have.\"",
